            <ArticleTitle>Understanding opioid tolerance in cancer pain.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1561-8; quiz 1569-70</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="PURPOSE/OBJECTIVES" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To review opioid tolerance in chronic cancer pain, define the phenomenon and its scope, review physiologic mechanisms, and discuss clinical strategies to identify and manage this complex issue.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DATA SOURCES" NlmCategory="METHODS">Review articles, case studies, original research, and published guidelines.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DATA SYNTHESIS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Novel therapies to prevent/reverse tolerance are being investigated with a possible future role for N-methyl-d-aspartate antagonists.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Greater nursing research is needed to identify patient risk factors for tolerance development and clinical measurement of the phenomenon. Understanding cellular mechanisms for tolerance may contribute to better management.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="IMPLICATIONS FOR NURSING PRACTICE" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Nursing knowledge of tolerance is important to provide the basis for accurate patient assessment, education, and pain management.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
